>
  <h2>Why a subcontractor should write minutes</h2>
  <p>
    The GC runs the meeting, so it is tempting to let the GC keep the record. The problem is that
    the GC’s minutes record what the GC heard, and on the items that cost you money — a delayed
    submittal review, an inspection that slipped, a directive to proceed before a change order is
    signed — what the GC heard and what you said are not always the same. Minutes you issue the same
    day, with a corrections-due date, put your version on the record first. If nobody objects, it
    stands. If they do, you find out in two days instead of two months.
  </p>
  <p>
    They also make your own week run. A sheet with ten action items, each with an owner and a date,
    is the foreman’s and the project manager’s to-do list until the next meeting, and the open items
    carry forward under their original numbers until someone closes them.
  </p>

  <h2>Field by field</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Meeting number and date</strong> — sequential per project (MTG-014). The number is the first
      half of every item number.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Attendees</strong> — name, company and role, with a present mark. List the expected absentees
      too; an action assigned to someone who was not there needs a phone call.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Item</strong> — meeting.item: 14.01, 14.02. A carried item keeps its number (12.03), so
      its age is visible.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Topic / discussion</strong> — the subject in a few words, then the facts and the decision
      in two to four lines.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Action, owner, due, status</strong> — the columns people actually read next week. One owner,
      one date. Status is Open or Closed.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Corrections due</strong> — the date after which the minutes stand as issued.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2>A worked example</h2>
  <p>
    The completed example is the fourteenth weekly coordination meeting on a medical office job,
    September 2, 2026, taken by the mechanical subcontractor’s project manager with eight people
    listed — the GC’s project manager and superintendent, the architect and her MEP engineer (by
    phone), the electrical foreman, and one absentee. Ten items, and every one is something the rest
    of the job’s paperwork can be traced to.
  </p>
  <p>
    Item 14.01 records the schedule status — Level 2 ductwork at 65%, hydronic piping at 55% — and
    the action is to issue schedule revision 2 the next day. Item 12.03, carried from meeting 12, is
    RFI-014 on the AHU-3 condensate drain: the revised drawing was issued August 28, so the action
    is to price it as change order CO-003 by September 14. Item 14.02 is the BMS controls submittal
    on its second revision, in review nine days, with the engineer’s return date tied to the
    September 14 controls start. Items 14.03 to 14.05 assign the RTU disconnects to the electrician,
    the Level 1 above-ceiling inspection to the superintendent, and pay application #3 to the sub —
    each with a name and a date. Page two closes the crane-day safety debrief — the same lift the
    <a href="/templates/toolbox-talk/">toolbox talk</a> and
    <a href="/templates/job-safety-analysis/">job safety analysis</a> examples cover — and the owner-furnished
    water heater credit (CO-002), and leaves two items open. Old business and new business summarize what
    carried and what was raised but not yet assigned.
  </p>
  <p>
    Read that page and you know the state of the mechanical scope on September 2 without having been
    in the trailer. That is the test of good minutes.
  </p>

  <h2>Minutes, RFIs and change orders</h2>
  <p>
    Minutes record that a decision was made; they do not make it contractual. When an item in the
    discussion column changes what you are building, price it and get it signed as a
    <a href="/templates/change-order/">change order</a>. When the item is a question about the
    documents, write the <a href="/templates/rfi/">RFI</a> and put its number in the minutes. When the
    item is a submittal, its dates belong on the <a href="/templates/submittal-log/"
      >submittal log</a
    >
    and the minutes just say where it stands. The minutes are the index; the other documents are the record.
    The same goes for the schedule — the minutes report the status, the
    <a href="/templates/construction-schedule/">construction schedule</a> carries the dates — and for
    the <a href="/templates/punch-list/">punch list</a> in the final weeks, when the open count is a standing
    agenda item.
  </p>

  <h2>Common mistakes</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Distributing minutes a week later, when nobody remembers enough to correct them.</li>
    <li>Actions without an owner, or with two owners — which means none.</li>
    <li>Renumbering carried items, so nobody can tell an item has been open for a month.</li>
    <li>
      Recording a directive to proceed in the minutes and treating that as the change order — see
      <a href="/blog/construction-change-order-management/">change order management</a> for what has to
      follow in writing.
    </li>
    <li>
      Writing a transcript. If the discussion column is longer than the action column, cut it.
    </li>
    <li>
      Skipping the meeting-day <a href="/templates/daily-report/">daily report</a> entry — the minutes
      record what was said; the daily report records what happened on site that day.
    </li>
  </ul>
</TemplatePage>
